<section class="ea-lab" data-design aria-label="Difference in differences sensitivity experiment">
  <header class="ea-lab-heading"><div><h3>What trend difference would change the decision?</h3><p>A hypothetical nonrandom training rollout.</p></div><span class="ea-live">Assumption analysis</span></header>
  <figure class="ea-figure"><svg data-design-plot viewBox="0 0 530 300" role="img" aria-label="Treated and comparison mean outcomes before and after, with an assumed untreated counterfactual range"><text x="30" y="50" font-size="14">The sensitivity figure requires JavaScript.</text></svg><figcaption>Solid blue: treated, 50 → 62. Gray: comparison, 40 → 46. Rust dashed: untreated treated-group counterfactual, 56 ± M. The shaded band is an assumption range, not a confidence interval.</figcaption></figure>
  <div class="ea-controls"><label class="ea-control">Maximum untreated trend difference, M <output data-m-value for="design-m">2.0</output><input id="design-m" data-design-m type="range" min="0" max="10" step=".5" value="2" /></label></div>
  <div class="ea-lab-readouts" aria-live="polite"><div>Compatible average effects<strong data-effect>…</strong></div><div>Net value per person<strong data-net>…</strong></div></div>
  <div class="ea-lab-foot"><p data-design-verdict></p><p>Each outcome unit is worth $100 in this toy decision; training costs $300 per person. These fixed values are assumptions. No spillovers, heterogeneous costs, or equilibrium effects are modeled.</p><button class="ea-button secondary" data-design-download>Download sensitivity table</button></div>
  <details><summary>Derivation and what this band leaves out</summary><p>Observed DiD = (62 − 50) − (46 − 40) = 6. Write δ for the treated group's untreated change minus the comparison group's untreated change. Then the treated-group average effect τ = 6 − δ, assuming no anticipation and stable composition. Restricting |δ| ≤ M gives τ ∈ [6 − M, 6 + M].</p><p>We treat the four means as known. There is no sampling uncertainty here. A defensible empirical interval must incorporate uncertainty in the estimated means and justify the bound. This algebraic sensitivity exercise is not an implementation of HonestDiD. The model also assumes a consistent treatment and no interference.</p></details>
</section>
